[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m model_response You are given two integers $a$ and $b$. You can perform a sequence of operations: during the first operation you choose one of these numbers and increase it by $1$; during the second operation you choose one of these numbers and increase it by $2$, and so on. You choose the number of these operations yourself.
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m For example, if $a = 1$ and $b = 3$, you can perform the following sequence of three operations:   add $1$ to $a$, then $a = 2$ and $b = 3$;  add $2$ to $b$, then $a = 2$ and $b = 5$;  add $3$ to $a$, then $a = 5$ and $b = 5$. 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m Calculate the minimum number of operations required to make $a$ and $b$ equal. 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m The first line contains one integer $t$ ($1 \le t \le 100$) — the number of test cases.
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m The only line of each test case contains two integers $a$ and $b$ ($1 \le a, b \le 10^9$).
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m For each test case print one integer — the minimum numbers of operations required to make $a$ and $b$ equal. 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m -----Example-----
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 3
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 1 3
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 11 11
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 30 20
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 3
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 0
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 4
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m First test case considered in the statement.
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m In the second test case integers $a$ and $b$ are already equal, so you don't need to perform any operations.
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m In the third test case you have to apply the first, the second, the third and the fourth operation to $b$ ($b$ turns into $20 + 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = 30$).
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m afterremove def solve():
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m     t = int(input())
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m     for _ in range(t):
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m         a, b = map(int, input().split())
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m         res = 0
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m         if a > b:
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m             a, b = b, a
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m         while a < b:
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m             a += 1
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m             b -= 1
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m             res += 1
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m         print(res)
