[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m model_responseBackground
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m There is a message that is circulating via public media that claims a reader can easily read a message where the inner letters of each words is scrambled, as long as the first and last letters remain the same and the word contains all the letters.
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m Another example shows that it is quite difficult to read the text where all the letters are reversed rather than scrambled.
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m In this kata we will make a generator that generates text in a similar pattern, but instead of scrambled or reversed, ours will be sorted alphabetically
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m Requirement
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m return a string where:
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 1) the first and last characters remain in original place for each word
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 2) characters between the first and last characters must be sorted alphabetically
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 3) punctuation should remain at the same place as it started, for example: shan't -> sahn't
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m Assumptions
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 1) words are seperated by single spaces
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 2) only spaces separate words, special characters do not,  for example: tik-tak -> tai-ktk
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 3) special characters do not take the position of the non special characters, for example: -dcba  ->  -dbca
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 4) for this kata puctuation is limited to 4 characters: hyphen(-), apostrophe('), comma(,) and period(.) 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 5) ignore capitalisation
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m for reference: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Typoglycemia
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 
