[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m model_response Solve the following coding problem using the programming language python:
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m You are given three integers x, y and n. Your task is to find the maximum integer k such that 0 ≤ k ≤ n that k mod x = y, where mod is modulo operation. Many programming languages use percent operator % to implement it.
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m In other words, with given x, y and n you need to find the maximum possible integer from 0 to n that has the remainder y modulo x.
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m You have to answer t independent test cases. It is guaranteed that such k exists for each test case.
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m The first line of the input contains one integer t (1 ≤ t ≤ 5 ⋅ 10^4) — the number of test cases. The next t lines contain test cases.
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m The only line of the test case contains three integers x, y and n (2 ≤ x ≤ 10^9;~ 0 ≤ y < x;~ y ≤ n ≤ 10^9).
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m It can be shown that such k always exists under the given constraints.
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m For each test case, print the answer — maximum non-negative integer k such that 0 ≤ k ≤ n and k mod x = y. It is guaranteed that the answer always exists.
[36m(WorkerDict pid=218553)[0m 
